    "title": "Artemis II making its return to Earth – Here’s what’s next",
    "text": "by: Eric Henrikson Posted: Apr 7, 2026 / 01:05 PM CDT Updated: Apr 7, 2026 / 03:07 PM CDT AUSTIN (KXAN) — After a history making flyby of the moon, the crew of Artemis II is on their way back to Earth. The next few days come with some extra science, plus prep for splashdown. On April 7, the Orion capsule, named Integrity, will formally leave lunar gravity and head toward Earth’s gravity well, according to NASA. The crew will have an extended rest period on this day. Artemis III explainer. Courtesy: NASA On April 8, the small engines aboard Integrity will fire to fine tune the spacecraft’s path on the return journey. Their goal is to land in the Pacific Ocean. With that in mind, April 8 will include performance tests of the life support systems, and antennas will see additional tests. April 9 will include final preparations for reentry and splashdown. Seats will be repositioned, loose equipment will be locked down and the crew will put on their reentry suits. April 10 is go-time. Integrity will fire its engines for reentry. The Orion capsule will enter the Earth’s atmosphere at about 25,000 miles per hour. It will be one of the fastest reentries ever made. Parachutes will slow the capsule to about 20mph ahead of splashdown in the Pacific. NASA aims to land west of Baja California or Southern California for easy recovery. NASA’s Space Launch System rocket carrying the Orion spacecraft launches on the Artemis I flight test, Wednesday, Nov. 16, 2022, from Launch Complex 39B at NASA’s Kennedy Space Center in Florida. (Credit: NASA/Bill Ingalls) Navy rescue ships will recover the astronauts, who will be flown to shore. Medical evaluations will occur as soon as the crew is taken aboard the Navy vessel. Following the mission, months of evaluations are scheduled. Engineers will analyze the Orion capsule and take what they learn to the next mission: Artemis III. Artemis III will occur in near-Earth orbit and will involve practicing several mechanical maneuvers. It is scheduled for 2027. Artemis IV will also happen in 2027. That mission is scheduled to land on the moon for the first time since the 1970s.",
